From 396320aefdea0313bb774589ee75d74d4d4d953e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 06:44:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add optional access logging to all site block types

Each block type's form gains a Logging accordion (above Advanced
options): an Enable logging switch that reveals a log level
(INFO/ERROR) and log format (Console/JSON) choice. When enabled, a
`log { output file ... }` block is written into the .conf, pointing at
<logs_dir>/<same base name as the .conf>.log. Disabling logging on save
deletes that log file; deleting a block does the same. If a block's
first site address changes and its .conf/.metadata sidecar get renamed,
its log file (and the path embedded in the .conf) are kept in sync with
the new name. Enabling logging without a logs directory configured in
Settings surfaces a form error instead of writing a broken path.

A hand-edited .conf's own log block (level/format) is picked up by the
fallback parser for the four structured block types, so it round-trips
through the app instead of being silently dropped on next save.

caddyfile.py stays pure (render_log_block + extract_logging, threaded
through every render_*/parse_conf_content), blocks.py owns the
filesystem side (log_path_for/delete_log_file/rename_log_file), and
views.py wires the two together, resolving the log file's name before
content is rendered since a new block's filename isn't known until its
site addresses are parsed.

Verified: unit checks on render_log_block/extract_logging and every
render_* function; full Flask test-client coverage of create/edit
(enable, disable-deletes-file, rename-carries-file-and-content),
delete-cleans-up-log, missing-log_dir error path, custom-block logging,
and hand-edited log-block round-trip, across all five block types;
jsdom check of the enable-switch show/hide wiring. pyflakes clean.
